---
import Guide from "../../../layouts/Guide.astro"
import Code from "../../../components/Code.astro"
import DocLink from "../../../components/DocLink.astro"
import InstallCommand from "./partials/InstallCommand.astro"
import NextSteps from "./partials/NextSteps.astro"
import PickAndChoose from "./partials/PickAndChoose.astro"
---

<Guide overline="Guide">
  <Fragment slot="title">Getting started</Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="preamble">
    OPUI ships first-class Vue components. Install the package, import what you
    need, and you're set.
  </Fragment>

  <section id="installation">
    <h2>Install via NPM</h2>
    <p>
      The components ship as compiled JavaScript with their types, so they work
      in any Vue setup without extra configuration, on the server too, Vite,
      Nuxt and Astro with <code>@astrojs/vue</code> included. They need Vue 3.5
      or later, so add <code>vue</code> if it isn't installed yet.
    </p>
    <InstallCommand />
  </section>

  <section id="css">
    <h2>Import the CSS</h2>
    <p>
      Vue components ship markup only - the CSS still has to be imported once,
      in the entry file that creates your app. Import everything:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`// src/main.ts
import { createApp } from "vue"
import "opui-css/css/imports.css"
import App from "./App.vue"

createApp(App).mount("#app")`}
      lang="ts"
    />
    <p>
      Or pick and choose in a CSS file, and import that file in
      <code>main.ts</code> instead:
    </p>
    <PickAndChoose file="src/styles/main.css">
      <p>
        In Nuxt, add the file to <code>css</code> in <code>nuxt.config.ts</code>
        instead.
      </p>
    </PickAndChoose>
    <Code
      code={`// src/main.ts
import "./styles/main.css"`}
      lang="ts"
    />
  </section>

  <section id="usage">
    <h2>How to use</h2>
    <Code
      code={`<script setup lang="ts">
import { Button } from "opui-css/vue"
</script>

<template>
  <Button variant="filled">Click me</Button>
</template>`}
      lang="vue"
    />
    <p>
      Containers take parts, such as <code>CardHeader</code> and
      <code>DialogFooter</code>. Other components take named slots for rich
      content, such as <code>end-text</code> on a <code>TextField</code>.
    </p>
    <NextSteps />
  </section>

  <section id="ssr">
    <h2>Server rendering</h2>
    <p>
      Without hydration (Astro with <code>@astrojs/vue</code> and no
      <code>client:*</code> directive, or <code>renderToString</code> on its
      own), every component renders complete HTML. The native controls work and
      submit with their form. A few things only update on the client:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/checkbox#indeterminate">Checkbox</DocLink>
        <code>indeterminate</code>: it's a DOM property, so the box looks
        unchecked. Or import <code>opui-css/css/js/checkbox.js</code> in the
        page.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/range#value">Range</DocLink> value: the
        <code>&lt;output&gt;</code> doesn't follow the thumb. The track fill is
        CSS, so it's fine.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>v-model</code> on
        <DocLink href="/components/checkbox">Checkbox</DocLink>,
        <DocLink href="/components/number-field">Number field</DocLink>,
        <DocLink href="/components/one-time-code">One-time code</DocLink>,
        <DocLink href="/components/radio">Radio</DocLink>,
        <DocLink href="/components/range">Range</DocLink>,
        <DocLink href="/components/rating">Rating</DocLink>,
        <DocLink href="/components/select">Select</DocLink>,
        <DocLink href="/components/switch">Switch</DocLink>,
        <DocLink href="/components/tabs">Tabs</DocLink>,
        <DocLink href="/components/text-field">Text field</DocLink>,
        <DocLink href="/components/textarea">Textarea</DocLink> and
        <DocLink href="/components/toggle">Toggle</DocLink>: the ref stays put.
        The native value still changes and submits.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Hydrate those (<code>client:load</code> in Astro) or use them in a
      client-rendered Vue app. Component pages say what needs hydration next to
      the browser support chips, and the API tables mark each prop with a
      crossed-out server icon.
    </p>
    <p>
      When the page hydrates, as in Nuxt, a value or choice the user changed
      before it did (typed, picked, filled in by a password manager or restored
      by the browser) stays: the form controls take it from the element instead
      of resetting it, and a form reset still returns to what the server
      rendered.
    </p>
  </section>
</Guide>
